PNT.registerModule({"id": "m10", "number": 10, "title": "Applied PNT Engineering (Capstone)", "subtitle": "Capstone: From mission need to an assured PNT solution", "version": "0.1", "objectives": ["Frame an operational problem in terms of Positioning, Navigation and Timing rather than immediately selecting technology.", "Identify mission-critical PNT capabilities, consumers, dependencies, interfaces and consequences of loss or deception.", "Trace the end-to-end PNT dependency chain, including upstream infrastructure and downstream consumers that may sit outside the immediate system boundary.", "Derive measurable performance and resilience requirements from operational scenarios.", "Construct a threat and common-mode analysis appropriate to the system boundary and environment.", "Develop and justify a multi-source resilient PNT architecture.", "Define normal, degraded, restricted, failover, holdover and recovery behaviours.", "Select suitable verification methods, reference truth, observables and acceptance criteria.", "Build a concise assurance argument that links claims to evidence, assumptions and residual risks.", "Communicate PNT engineering decisions clearly to technical and non-technical stakeholders.", "Challenge apparently attractive solutions that do not trace back to mission need or independently evidenced requirements.", "Evaluate emerging or supplier-proposed PNT technologies using evidence of maturity, operating envelope, integration dependencies and assurance rather than novelty or headline claims."], "lessons": [{"id": "the-capstone-think-like-a-pnt-engineer", "title": "The Capstone: Think Like a PNT Engineer", "html": "<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">CAPSTONE MODULE</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>This module consolidates Modules 1-9. It introduces very little new theory. Instead, it asks the learner to apply PNT fundamentals, threats, resilience engineering, systems engineering, verification and assurance to realistic operational problems.</p></div></aside>\n<p>A PNT engineer is rarely handed a clean requirement that says exactly which receiver, clock or navigation source to buy. The real problem begins with an operational need, an environment, constraints, threats and consequences. The engineering task is to determine what PNT capability is actually required, design an architecture that can provide it, and define evidence that justifies confidence in the resulting system.</p>\n<p>The purpose of this module is therefore integration. The learner will move repeatedly through the complete engineering chain developed across the course.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m10-section\"><figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: The Capstone: Think Like a PNT Engineer\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m10-image1.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure></h2>\n<h2 id=\"m10-learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Frame an operational problem in terms of Positioning, Navigation and Timing rather than immediately selecting technology.</p></li>\n<li><p>Identify mission-critical PNT capabilities, consumers, dependencies, interfaces and consequences of loss or deception.</p></li>\n<li><p>Trace the end-to-end PNT dependency chain, including upstream infrastructure and downstream consumers that may sit outside the immediate system boundary.</p></li>\n<li><p>Derive measurable performance and resilience requirements from operational scenarios.</p></li>\n<li><p>Construct a threat and common-mode analysis appropriate to the system boundary and environment.</p></li>\n<li><p>Develop and justify a multi-source resilient PNT architecture.</p></li>\n<li><p>Define normal, degraded, restricted, failover, holdover and recovery behaviours.</p></li>\n<li><p>Select suitable verification methods, reference truth, observables and acceptance criteria.</p></li>\n<li><p>Build a concise assurance argument that links claims to evidence, assumptions and residual risks.</p></li>\n<li><p>Communicate PNT engineering decisions clearly to technical and non-technical stakeholders.</p></li>\n<li><p>Challenge apparently attractive solutions that do not trace back to mission need or independently evidenced requirements.</p></li>\n<li><p>Evaluate emerging or supplier-proposed PNT technologies using evidence of maturity, operating envelope, integration dependencies and assurance rather than novelty or headline claims.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m10-what-good-looks-like\">What good looks like</h2>\n<p>There is deliberately no single correct technology stack in the exercises. A strong answer is traceable, measurable and threat-aware. It explains why a design is appropriate for the mission, identifies its assumptions and limitations, and specifies how important claims will be verified.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">ENGINEER INSIGHT</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>The quality of the reasoning matters more than the number of PNT technologies included. Adding sources without understanding independence, failure modes, interfaces and operational behaviour can increase complexity without increasing resilience.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "a-repeatable-applied-pnt-engineering-method", "title": "A Repeatable Applied PNT Engineering Method", "html": "<p>1. Define the mission and operating context. What must the user or system achieve, where, for how long and under what constraints?</p>\n<p>2. Identify the PNT dependency chain. Which functions need position, velocity, navigation, orientation or time? Which consumers use them, and what upstream sources, infrastructure, networks, clocks, services and interfaces does the capability ultimately depend on?</p>\n<p>3. Define consequences. What happens if PNT is unavailable, degraded, stale or convincingly wrong?</p>\n<p>4. Derive measurable requirements. State performance, integrity, availability, continuity, latency, outage, holdover, detection and recovery needs where applicable.</p>\n<p>5. Define the threat and failure model. Include environmental, accidental, malicious, cyber, equipment and dependency failures relevant to the mission.</p>\n<p>6. Design the architecture. Select sources and resilience mechanisms because they address requirements and failure modes, not because they are fashionable.</p>\n<p>7. Define operational behaviour. Specify monitoring, isolation, failover, degraded modes, user alerts and controlled recovery.</p>\n<p>8. Plan verification and validation. Decide what must be inspected, analysed, demonstrated or tested, with suitable truth references and acceptance criteria.</p>\n<p>9. Build the assurance argument. Link critical claims to evidence and make assumptions, gaps and residual risks visible.</p>"}, {"id": "worked-case-study-autonomous-coastal-surveillance-craft", "title": "Worked Case Study: Autonomous Coastal Surveillance Craft", "html": "<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">SCENARIO</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>A small autonomous surface craft conducts coastal surveillance for up to 8 hours. It follows pre-planned routes, reports observations to a control station and must avoid navigational hazards. It normally uses multi-constellation GNSS. The craft may operate in areas where GNSS interference has been reported. Communications may be intermittent. Size, weight, power and cost are constrained. A human operator can supervise but cannot continuously pilot the craft.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m10-step-1-mission-and-pnt-dependency\">Step 1: Mission and PNT dependency</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Mission function</strong></th>\n<th><strong>PNT information</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Why it matters</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Consequence if wrong</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Route following</td>\n<td>Position, velocity, heading</td>\n<td>Stay inside planned corridor</td>\n<td>Hazard approach, mission deviation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Hazard avoidance</td>\n<td>Position + local sensing</td>\n<td>Relate detected objects to own movement</td>\n<td>Collision or unsafe manoeuvre</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Sensor geolocation</td>\n<td>Position + time + orientation</td>\n<td>Locate observations</td>\n<td>Mislocated intelligence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Return / recovery</td>\n<td>Position and navigation</td>\n<td>Reach recovery point</td>\n<td>Loss of platform</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Event correlation</td>\n<td>Accurate time</td>\n<td>Correlate observations and logs</td>\n<td>Incorrect sequence or attribution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Operator awareness</td>\n<td>PNT state + integrity</td>\n<td>Understand craft state</td>\n<td>Unsafe intervention or false confidence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The analysis immediately shows why “the craft needs GPS” is an inadequate requirement. Different mission functions consume different PNT properties and have different consequences when those properties become unreliable.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m10-step-2-operational-modes-and-consequences\">Step 2: Operational modes and consequences</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>PNT condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Engineering interpretation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Expected system behaviour</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Nominal</td>\n<td>Sources agree and required performance is met</td>\n<td>Normal autonomous operation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Degraded</td>\n<td>Performance reduced but bounded and trustworthy</td>\n<td>Continue with restrictions; expose uncertainty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Suspect</td>\n<td>One or more sources inconsistent or integrity uncertain</td>\n<td>Isolate suspect contribution; increase monitoring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Restricted</td>\n<td>Remaining capability insufficient for full mission</td>\n<td>Reduce speed / mission scope; seek safer operating state</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Unavailable / unsafe</td>\n<td>No trustworthy solution for required function</td>\n<td>Execute predefined safe behaviour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>Primary source returns but trust not yet established</td>\n<td>Revalidate before controlled reintegration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "deriving-requirements-from-the-scenario", "title": "Deriving Requirements from the Scenario", "html": "<p>The numbers below are illustrative training values, not universal maritime requirements. Their purpose is to demonstrate the form of a verifiable requirement.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>ID</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Illustrative requirement</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Rationale / source</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-01</td>\n<td>During nominal route following, horizontal position error shall remain ≤ 5 m for at least 95% of mission time.</td>\n<td>Mission corridor and navigation analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT-02</td>\n<td>Following loss of usable GNSS, the navigation solution shall remain within 20 m horizontal error for at least 120 s.</td>\n<td>Time required to transition to restricted behaviour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-03</td>\n<td>A source inconsistency capable of causing &gt; 15 m position error shall be detected and declared within 5 s under defined test conditions.</td>\n<td>Limits exposure to misleading navigation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT-04</td>\n<td>A source declared suspect shall not contribute to the operational navigation solution until revalidation criteria are satisfied.</td>\n<td>Prevents contamination of fused solution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-05</td>\n<td>The PNT service shall expose solution age, uncertainty, integrity state and current operating mode to the autonomy function.</td>\n<td>Enables application-level decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT-06</td>\n<td>After restoration of a previously suspect GNSS source, reintegration shall require sustained agreement with independent sources for a defined validation period.</td>\n<td>Controlled recovery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-07</td>\n<td>Loss of the primary communications link shall not by itself remove the craft’s locally available navigation capability.</td>\n<td>Avoid hidden communications dependency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">REQUIREMENT QUALITY CHECK</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>Can the requirement be observed or analysed? Is the threshold measurable? Are the conditions defined? Is the duration clear? Is the consuming function identified? Can an engineer explain where the number came from? If not, the requirement probably needs more work.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "threat-and-common-mode-analysis", "title": "Threat and Common-Mode Analysis", "html": "<p>The threat model should be proportionate to the mission. It is not simply a catalogue of every possible PNT threat. It asks which credible conditions can harm the critical capabilities identified above.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Potential effect</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Detection opportunity</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Design response</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GNSS denial / severe interference</td>\n<td>GNSS measurements unavailable or degraded</td>\n<td>Signal quality, loss of tracking, cross-source monitoring</td>\n<td>Bridge using inertial / local sensors; restrict mission as uncertainty grows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Misleading GNSS</td>\n<td>Plausible but incorrect position/time</td>\n<td>Cross-source residuals, dynamics checks, integrity monitoring</td>\n<td>Isolate suspect GNSS; avoid contaminating fused solution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>IMU bias / sensor fault</td>\n<td>Growing navigation error</td>\n<td>Residuals, calibration status, sensor health</td>\n<td>Fault isolation; bound uncertainty; use other sources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Shared antenna / RF front-end failure</td>\n<td>Multiple GNSS constellations lost together</td>\n<td>Hardware health, simultaneous source loss</td>\n<td>Do not count constellations as independent hardware paths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Communications loss</td>\n<td>Remote corrections / supervision unavailable</td>\n<td>Link state</td>\n<td>Preserve local capability; enter defined autonomous mode</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Software / configuration defect</td>\n<td>Incorrect fusion or thresholds</td>\n<td>Self-test, logs, configuration integrity, verification</td>\n<td>Controlled configuration; independent checks; safe fallback</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Power subsystem fault</td>\n<td>Multiple PNT components lost</td>\n<td>Power health</td>\n<td>Partition critical supplies where justified</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"m10-common-mode-challenge\">Common-mode challenge</h2>\n<p>Suppose the craft contains two GNSS receivers, both connected to the same antenna, powered from the same converter and processed by the same navigation computer. It has hardware redundancy, but several common dependencies remain. A single antenna fault, RF interference event, power fault or navigation-software defect may defeat both paths.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">CAPSTONE HABIT</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>Whenever you see “backup”, ask: backup against which failure? Then trace antenna, power, software, network, clock, correction service, reference frame, installation and operator dependencies.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "candidate-resilient-architecture", "title": "Candidate Resilient Architecture", "html": "<p>A defensible candidate architecture could combine complementary sources and explicit integrity management. This is not presented as the only correct design.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Candidate Resilient Architecture\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m10-image2.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m10-why-this-architecture-is-stronger-than-two-gnss-receivers\">Why this architecture is stronger than “two GNSS receivers”</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>It separates source acquisition from trust decisions.</p></li>\n<li><p>It uses sources with different characteristics rather than assuming constellation diversity removes common-mode risk.</p></li>\n<li><p>It monitors health and consistency before fusion.</p></li>\n<li><p>It propagates uncertainty and integrity state to the application.</p></li>\n<li><p>It contains explicit mode-management and recovery logic.</p></li>\n<li><p>It allows the application to change behaviour when PNT capability changes.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "walkthrough-hard-gnss-loss", "title": "Walkthrough: Hard GNSS Loss", "html": "<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Walkthrough: Hard GNSS Loss\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m10-image3.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m10-engineering-questions\">Engineering questions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>What exactly triggers the loss declaration?</p></li>\n<li><p>How long can the inertial solution bridge the outage before the mission threshold is exceeded?</p></li>\n<li><p>What information tells the autonomy function that uncertainty is growing?</p></li>\n<li><p>What safe behaviour is possible without trustworthy absolute position?</p></li>\n<li><p>What conditions must be satisfied before normal operation resumes?</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "walkthrough-plausible-but-wrong-gnss", "title": "Walkthrough: Plausible but Wrong GNSS", "html": "<p>This is the more demanding case. GNSS may continue to produce apparently healthy measurements while the solution gradually diverges. A design that only detects complete signal loss can therefore fail dangerously.</p>\n<p>1. Independent measurements begin to disagree with the GNSS-derived solution.</p>\n<p>2. The integrity function evaluates residuals, dynamics and source-health indicators against configured thresholds.</p>\n<p>3. The system declares GNSS suspect before its error can exceed the mission-defined exposure limit, if the detection requirement is met.</p>\n<p>4. Suspect measurements are isolated from the operational solution. The system does not simply average them with trusted sources.</p>\n<p>5. The PNT manager changes operating mode and exposes the new integrity state and uncertainty to the autonomy function.</p>\n<p>6. If the remaining sources cannot sustain the required performance, the application progressively restricts operation.</p>\n<p>7. When GNSS later appears normal, it remains quarantined until revalidation criteria are met.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Walkthrough: Plausible but Wrong GNSS\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m10-image4.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">IMPORTANT</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>Redundancy without fault isolation can make deception worse. A fusion algorithm that continues weighting a plausible but corrupted source may produce a smooth, precise-looking and incorrect solution.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "turning-the-design-into-verification-evidence", "title": "Turning the Design into Verification Evidence", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Req.</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Method</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Scenario / stimulus</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Observable</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example acceptance evidence</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-02</td>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>Controlled removal of usable GNSS while following representative trajectory</td>\n<td>Truth-referenced horizontal error vs time</td>\n<td>Error ≤20 m for ≥120 s; mode transitions recorded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT-03</td>\n<td>Test + analysis</td>\n<td>Controlled misleading-source scenario within authorised test environment</td>\n<td>Injected error, detection time, alert state</td>\n<td>Declared within 5 s for defined test set; misses/false alerts reported</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-04</td>\n<td>Inspection + test</td>\n<td>Force source to suspect state</td>\n<td>Fusion inputs, source state, PNT output</td>\n<td>Suspect source contribution excluded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT-05</td>\n<td>Inspection + test</td>\n<td>Exercise nominal and degraded modes</td>\n<td>Interface messages / logs</td>\n<td>Age, uncertainty, integrity and mode exposed correctly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-06</td>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>Restore previously suspect source</td>\n<td>Agreement metrics, quarantine duration, reintegration state</td>\n<td>No reintegration before all defined criteria pass</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT-07</td>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>Remove primary communications link</td>\n<td>Local PNT output and autonomy state</td>\n<td>Required local navigation remains available</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Testing intentional RF interference or deceptive navigation signals must only be conducted in authorised, controlled facilities using lawful methods. Simulation, recorded data, laboratory signal generation or other approved techniques can provide repeatable evidence without uncontrolled transmission.</p>"}, {"id": "from-evidence-to-a-pnt-assurance-argument", "title": "From Evidence to a PNT Assurance Argument", "html": "<p>A pile of test reports is not automatically an assurance case. Assurance explains why the evidence is sufficient to support important claims for the intended application and environment.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: From Evidence to a PNT Assurance Argument\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m10-image5.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">ENGINEER INSIGHT</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>Assurance should be bounded. “Resilient PNT” is too broad. A defensible claim states the mission, environment, threat or failure conditions, performance envelope and evidence on which confidence depends.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "design-review-questions-an-sme-should-ask", "title": "Design Review: Questions an SME Should Ask", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Area</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Questions</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Mission</td>\n<td>Which PNT functions are mission-critical? What happens if they are absent, stale or wrong?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Requirements</td>\n<td>Where did the thresholds come from? Are duration, environment and probability/availability conditions defined?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Architecture</td>\n<td>Which sources are genuinely independent? What dependencies are shared?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Integrity</td>\n<td>How does the system know when a source or solution should not be trusted?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Failover</td>\n<td>What triggers transition? Is failover automatic, manual or both? What happens during the transition?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Holdover</td>\n<td>How does error grow with outage duration? At what point is the capability no longer operationally acceptable?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Application</td>\n<td>Does the consumer receive uncertainty, freshness, integrity and mode information, or only coordinates/time?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>How is a returning source revalidated? What prevents oscillation between modes?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Verification</td>\n<td>What reference truth, scenarios, configurations and environmental conditions were used?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assurance</td>\n<td>What evidence supports the claim, and what remains assumed or unverified?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "common-capstone-failure-modes", "title": "Common Capstone Failure Modes", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Weak approach</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Why it is weak</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Better engineering response</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>“Use GPS + Galileo + BeiDou for resilience.”</td>\n<td>Shared RF environment and other dependencies remain.</td>\n<td>Analyse failure independence and add complementary sources where justified.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>“The receiver is accurate to 1 m.”</td>\n<td>No conditions, probability, integrity or evidence.</td>\n<td>Define measurable performance envelope and evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>“If GPS fails, switch to INS.”</td>\n<td>No trigger, transition, drift bound or duration.</td>\n<td>Specify detection, failover, uncertainty growth and degraded-mode thresholds.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>“Sensor fusion makes it resilient.”</td>\n<td>Fusion can combine bad data as well as good data.</td>\n<td>Define health monitoring, FDI/FDE and integrity-aware fusion.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>“The system passed the demo.”</td>\n<td>A demonstration may not challenge failure modes or boundaries.</td>\n<td>Use traceable IADT evidence and negative/degraded testing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>“GNSS has recovered, switch back.”</td>\n<td>Returning data may still be wrong or unstable.</td>\n<td>Use quarantine, revalidation and controlled reintegration.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>“Backup service is independent.”</td>\n<td>It may share GNSS, network, power, clock or supplier dependencies.</td>\n<td>Trace upstream dependencies and common-mode paths.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "emerging-technology-evidence-challenge", "title": "Emerging Technology Evidence Challenge", "html": "<p>A new PNT technology can be attractive because it promises better accuracy, availability or resilience. Practitioner-level engineering requires the learner to separate technical potential from evidence that the capability is sufficiently mature, integrated and assured for the mission. This exercise can be used as an optional extension to the capstone or as a facilitated design-review activity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m10-scenario\">Scenario</h2>\n<p>A supplier proposes an emerging PNT capability as an additional resilient source for your architecture. The technology may be based on LEO signals, advanced inertial sensing, quantum sensing, AI/ML-assisted estimation, signals of opportunity or another developing approach. The supplier provides promising headline performance but limited evidence from your intended operating environment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m10-your-task\">Your task</h2>\n<p>1. State which mission requirement or failure mode the technology is intended to address.</p>\n<p>2. Identify the evidence needed to substantiate its claimed accuracy, availability, integrity, continuity or holdover performance.</p>\n<p>3. Assess maturity: what has been demonstrated, in which environment, on what configuration, and at what scale?</p>\n<p>4. Trace the dependency chain introduced by the technology, including external infrastructure, communications, power, software, data, reference services and supplier dependencies.</p>\n<p>5. Identify credible common-mode failures with the existing architecture.</p>\n<p>6. Define the verification or trial evidence required before the technology could be trusted for the intended role.</p>\n<p>7. State a bounded engineering conclusion: suitable now, suitable only for trial/augmentation, or insufficiently evidenced for the proposed role, and explain what evidence would change that conclusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m10-engineer-insight\">ENGINEER INSIGHT</h2>\n<p>Technology readiness is not the same as mission readiness. A technology can be scientifically credible or successfully demonstrated while still lacking the environmental evidence, integration maturity, support model, security evidence or failure-characterisation needed for operational use.</p>"}, {"id": "learner-capstone-exercise", "title": "Learner Capstone Exercise", "html": "<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">YOUR SCENARIO</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>A deployable command post must operate for 72 hours at a remote site. It needs accurate time for secure communications, event correlation, distributed computing and sensor data. GNSS is normally available, but the deployment may experience intermittent GNSS interference and loss of external network connectivity. The site has limited power and communications bandwidth. Equipment must be transportable and support rapid setup. The customer states: “We need resilient timing even if GPS is jammed.”</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m10-your-task-1\">Your task</h2>\n<p>Prepare a concise PNT engineering response. Use diagrams and tables where useful. Do not begin by choosing a product. Your submission should contain the following eight sections:</p>\n<p>1. Mission and dependency analysis: identify timing consumers, criticality and consequences of unavailable, degraded or misleading time. Trace the upstream timing dependency chain, including external references, communications, power and distribution dependencies.</p>\n<p>2. System boundary and assumptions: show what is inside/outside the proposed PNT/timing system and identify external dependencies.</p>\n<p>3. Requirements: propose measurable timing, availability, holdover, detection, failover and recovery requirements. State assumptions behind any illustrative numbers.</p>\n<p>4. Threat and failure analysis: identify credible conditions and at least three common-mode risks.</p>\n<p>5. Architecture: propose a layered timing architecture with source monitoring, selection/fusion, local holdover, integrity state and distribution to consumers.</p>\n<p>6. Operational behaviour: describe normal operation, GNSS loss, suspect GNSS, extended outage and controlled recovery.</p>\n<p>7. Verification strategy: map the most important requirements to IADT methods, reference truth, observables and acceptance criteria.</p>\n<p>8. Assurance statement: state what you could claim if the proposed evidence passes, plus key assumptions, limitations and residual risks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m10-minimum-artefacts\">Minimum artefacts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>One context / dependency-chain diagram showing upstream dependencies and downstream timing consumers.</p></li>\n<li><p>One logical PNT architecture diagram.</p></li>\n<li><p>A requirements table with at least eight measurable requirements.</p></li>\n<li><p>A threat and common-mode table.</p></li>\n<li><p>A mode / failover / recovery description.</p></li>\n<li><p>A verification matrix covering at least six requirements.</p></li>\n<li><p>A short assurance argument.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "capstone-assessment-rubric", "title": "Capstone Assessment Rubric", "html": "<p>This rubric can support a future Daintta PNT Practitioner assessment. It deliberately rewards engineering traceability and judgement rather than a particular technology choice.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Assessment area</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Weight</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What strong evidence looks like</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Mission &amp; dependency understanding</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>Correctly distinguishes timing need from technology; identifies consumers, consequences and operating context.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Requirements engineering</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>Measurable, bounded, traceable requirements covering performance and resilience.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Threat &amp; common-mode analysis</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>Credible threat model; identifies hidden shared dependencies and misleading-data risk.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Architecture &amp; rationale</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>Layered, proportionate design; source independence considered; monitoring and integrity included.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Operational behaviour</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>Clear normal, degraded, failover, holdover and recovery states with application consequences.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Verification strategy</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>Appropriate IADT methods, reference truth, observables, acceptance criteria and coverage.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Assurance &amp; communication</td>\n<td>5%</td>\n<td>Claims are bounded by evidence; assumptions and residual risks are explicit and clearly communicated.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">ASSESSMENT PRINCIPLE</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>A sophisticated architecture with weak requirements and no credible evidence should not outscore a simpler architecture that is well justified, measurable and demonstrably fit for the mission.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "module-summary", "title": "Module Summary", "html": "<ul>\n<li><p>Applied PNT engineering starts with mission need, not a preferred technology.</p></li>\n<li><p>PNT dependencies must be understood at the consuming-function level, including the consequence of absent, degraded, stale or misleading information.</p></li>\n<li><p>Resilience requirements should be measurable, threat-aware and time-bounded.</p></li>\n<li><p>Multiple sources only improve resilience when their failure modes and dependencies provide useful independence.</p></li>\n<li><p>Integrity monitoring, FDI/FDE, uncertainty propagation and application-level mode management are central to trustworthy PNT.</p></li>\n<li><p>Failover and holdover must have defined triggers, performance envelopes and expiry conditions.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recovery requires revalidation; source return does not automatically restore trust.</p></li>\n<li><p>Verification should combine appropriate Inspection, Analysis, Demonstration and Test evidence.</p></li>\n<li><p>Assurance is an argument supported by evidence, not a collection of successful demonstrations.</p></li>\n<li><p>The system boundary does not remove external dependencies from assurance; important upstream and downstream dependencies should be visible, evidenced or explicitly treated as assumptions and residual risk.</p></li>\n<li><p>Emerging PNT technologies should be assessed against mission need, technology maturity, dependency, operating-envelope evidence and verification readiness rather than novelty alone.</p></li>\n<li><p>A strong PNT engineer can explain the mission, requirement, architecture choice, limitation and evidence chain in plain language.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">COURSE TAKEAWAY</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>PNT engineering is not the art of choosing a navigation receiver. It is the disciplined engineering of trustworthy Positioning, Navigation and Timing capability for a defined mission, including what happens when the environment, sources or system stop behaving normally.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "key-terms", "title": "Key Terms", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Meaning in this course</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Assurance</td>\n<td>Structured confidence, supported by evidence and argument, that defined critical properties are satisfied for the intended use.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Common-mode failure</td>\n<td>A condition capable of defeating supposedly redundant elements through a shared dependency or failure mechanism.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Degraded mode</td>\n<td>An operating state in which capability is reduced but remains bounded and intentionally managed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>FDI / FDE</td>\n<td>Fault Detection and Isolation / Fault Detection and Exclusion, mechanisms used to identify and prevent suspect information influencing the solution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Holdover / bridging</td>\n<td>Maintaining a required capability for a bounded period after loss of an external reference.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Integrity</td>\n<td>Confidence that information can be trusted for the intended operation, including timely indication when it should not be used.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT dependency</td>\n<td>A mission, system or function’s reliance on position, navigation, velocity, orientation or timing information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>Controlled restoration of normal capability and trust after disruption or compromise.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Reference truth</td>\n<td>A measurement or source sufficiently accurate and traceable to evaluate the system under test.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Residual risk</td>\n<td>Risk remaining after implemented controls and mitigations are considered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT dependency chain</td>\n<td>End-to-end set of sources, infrastructure, services, interfaces and consumers on which a PNT capability depends.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Technology maturity</td>\n<td>Degree to which a technology has been demonstrated, integrated and evidenced for the intended environment and operational role.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Operating envelope</td>\n<td>Defined environmental, dynamic, threat and configuration conditions within which a performance or assurance claim applies.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "further-learning-and-source-material", "title": "Further Learning and Source Material", "html": "<p>The capstone consolidates the authoritative and open-source material used throughout the programme. Learners should use the following for deeper study and to challenge or extend their capstone assumptions:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Source</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Why it matters</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>UK Government: Positioning, Navigation and Timing overview</td>\n<td>Current UK policy context, national dependencies and Framework for Greater PNT Resilience.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>UK Space Agency: Space PNT / technical delivery material</td>\n<td>UK system-of-systems direction and ongoing resilient PNT capability development.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Royal Institute of Navigation: PNT Resilience Checklist</td>\n<td>Practical dependency, outage, mitigation and preparedness questions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>RIN: Impacts of GNSS Interference on Maritime Safety (2026)</td>\n<td>Applied evidence of GNSS interference, operational dependency and mitigation issues.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>RethinkPNT: PNT System Resilience</td>\n<td>Use cases, requirements, resilience functions, redundancy, verification and PNT assurance case concepts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>DHS/CISA: Resilient PNT Conformance Framework</td>\n<td>Outcome-based resilient behaviour, equipment boundaries, evaluation and test-plan concepts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>DHS: Best Practices for Resilient PNT</td>\n<td>Risk management, testing and evaluation best-practice guidance for PNT-dependent critical infrastructure.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>NIST IR 8323 Rev. 2 public draft</td>\n<td>Cybersecurity-oriented PNT risk management across Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Royal Institute of Navigation: Introduction to PNT in the 21st Century</td>\n<td>Complementary professional learning covering PNT foundations, resilient PNT, alternative technologies, time/frequency distribution and emerging PNT topics.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Where standards, policies or draft publications evolve, learners should confirm the latest applicable version before using them as contractual or assurance requirements.</p>"}, {"id": "programme-completion-and-next-step", "title": "Programme Completion and Next Step", "html": "<p>Completion of Modules 1-10 provides the knowledge foundation for the proposed Daintta PNT Practitioner pathway. The capstone is intended to demonstrate integrated understanding, but completion of training alone should not be represented as external professional certification or as evidence of unrestricted practical competence.</p>\n<p>A stronger internal practitioner award can combine successful knowledge assessment with a reviewed capstone submission. A future advanced level could additionally require demonstrated application on a real Daintta or client PNT engagement, reviewed by an experienced PNT subject-matter expert.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">END OF FOUNDATION &amp; PRACTITIONER LEARNING PROGRAMME</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>You should now be able to participate constructively in technical PNT conversations, identify weak or unsupported resilience claims, contribute to PNT requirements and architecture discussions, and understand the evidence needed to substantiate PNT performance and resilience.</p></div></aside>"}], "practice": [{"n": 1, "prompt": "What should normally be the first step in an applied PNT engineering problem?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Select the most accurate receiver"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Define the mission, operating context and PNT dependency"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Choose three GNSS constellations"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Build a test rig"}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Engineering begins with the mission and the capability the user needs. Technology selection follows requirements and risk analysis."}, {"n": 2, "prompt": "Which statement best describes a strong resilience requirement?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The system shall be resilient to GNSS loss"}, {"id": "B", "text": "The system shall use an INS backup"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Following loss of usable GNSS, horizontal error shall remain within a defined bound for a defined duration under stated conditions"}, {"id": "D", "text": "The system shall recover quickly"}], "answer": ["C"], "rationale": "It defines the event, measurable performance, duration and conditions. The others are vague or prematurely prescribe a solution."}, {"n": 3, "prompt": "Which can create common-mode failure?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Shared antenna"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Shared power supply"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Shared navigation software"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Independent physical principles with separate infrastructure"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C"], "rationale": "Redundant components can still share antenna, power, software, network, clock or other dependencies. Genuine independence must be analysed."}, {"n": 4, "prompt": "Why is plausible-but-wrong PNT especially important?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "It always causes the receiver to switch off"}, {"id": "B", "text": "The application may continue acting confidently on misleading information"}, {"id": "C", "text": "It only affects timing"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It is identical to a clean loss of service"}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Misleading PNT can preserve apparent availability while damaging integrity, allowing unsafe decisions to continue."}, {"n": 5, "prompt": "What should happen when a previously suspect source returns?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Immediately give it full weighting"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Ignore it forever"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Revalidate it against defined criteria before controlled reintegration"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Restart the entire platform"}], "answer": ["C"], "rationale": "Recovery is a controlled engineering state. Returning data should be monitored and revalidated before trust is restored."}, {"n": 6, "prompt": "Useful PNT service outputs to a consuming application can include:", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Position/time"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Uncertainty"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Integrity / health state"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Solution age and operating mode"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "rationale": "The consumer often needs quality and trust information as well as the PNT value itself."}, {"n": 7, "prompt": "What is the main purpose of a verification matrix?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Replace system requirements"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Link requirements to methods, scenarios, evidence and acceptance criteria"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Rank GNSS constellations"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Record only successful tests"}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "The matrix makes the requirements-to-evidence chain visible and supports coverage, traceability and assurance."}, {"n": 8, "prompt": "Why can an assurance claim not simply say “the system is resilient”?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Resilience cannot be tested"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Resilience is meaningful only when bounded by mission, conditions, threats, performance and evidence"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Only GNSS systems need assurance"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Assurance applies only to safety cases"}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Resilience is mission- and threat-relative. A useful claim states the operating envelope and evidence supporting it."}, {"n": 9, "prompt": "A strong capstone architecture should consider:", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Source independence"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Fault detection and isolation"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Degraded-mode behaviour"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Recovery and revalidation"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "rationale": "Resilience depends on the whole behaviour of the system, not merely the number of sources."}, {"n": 10, "prompt": "Which response best demonstrates practitioner-level PNT thinking?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "“This product supports five constellations, so it is resilient.”"}, {"id": "B", "text": "“We selected the most expensive receiver.”"}, {"id": "C", "text": "“Here are the mission requirements, credible failure modes, architecture rationale, operational transitions and evidence needed to substantiate the claims.”"}, {"id": "D", "text": "“The supplier says it is spoof-proof.”"}], "answer": ["C"], "rationale": "Practitioner-level reasoning connects operational need, requirements, risk, architecture, behaviour and evidence."}]});
